[quote="ricky.pattillo"]

The Informant&#39;s game text is &quot;When a Hero card is played, play the top card of the villain deck.&quot;. &nbsp;Did &quot;enters play&quot; become interchangeable with &quot;played&quot;? &nbsp;<a href="https://greaterthangames.com/comment/59875#comment-59875My">https://greaterthangames.com/comment/59875#comment-59875My</a> understanding is that played triggers happen before a card enters play, or any enters-play triggers. &nbsp;

[/quote]
&#39;Enters play&#39; is (and always was) interchangeable with &#39;played&#39;. &nbsp;&#39;Played&#39; triggers (like in the referenced comment) happen after the card has entered play, but before it&#39;s text begins to resolve, as described in the comment you linked to, and as described by the fireside chats. &nbsp;Except that static effects (like Heroic interception&#39;s damage immunity) don&#39;t resolve at all, they&#39;re just &#39;on&#39; all the time that the card is in play.

&nbsp;

Am I correct in guessing that you play Magic? &nbsp;In MtG, &#39;enters play&#39; and &#39;played&#39; are seperate because of the existence of the stack. &nbsp;But SotM doesn&#39;t have a stack, so it combines the two, and SotM&#39;s language hasn&#39;t been completely standardized yet, thus occasionally using two terms for the same thing.

[quote="AlexxKay"]

&nbsp;
<div class="bb-quote"><b>Arcanist Lupus wrote:</b><br /><blockquote class="bb-quote-body">But SotM doesn&#39;t have a stack
&nbsp;
</blockquote></div>
I have seen this statement made many times, and I can&#39;t agree with it.&nbsp; It may not acknowledge the existence of a stack anywhere in the rules (or offical rulings), but it certainly behaves like a stack.&nbsp; I&#39;ve been playing a lot in the app lately with a combination of Argent Adept and Grandpa Legacy handing powers back and forth to each other, and I&#39;m certainly glad that the app can keep track of all the nested state (i.e. stack) that&#39;s involved there.&nbsp; If it looks like a duck and it quacks like a duck...I don&#39;t deny that there are significant differences between MtG and SotM, but &quot;having a stack&quot; is not one of them.

[/quote]

Maybe it does have a stack, but not the same way MtG does.&nbsp; In Magic, the stack is discrete on the ability level (entire abilities resolve before the game state reacts to the changes) and there is time to respond between the triggering of abilties and their resolution. &nbsp;In SotM, the &#39;stack&#39; is discrete on an action level, and abilties resolve as soon as they are triggered. &nbsp;The second one matters less (since SotM doesn&#39;t have &#39;instant speed&#39; actions (yet)), but these differences make the SotM system sufficiently distinct from Magic&#39;s stack that trying to understand SotM timing using Magic&#39;s stack will frequently provide the wrong answer, which is why we try to discourage people from saying that SotM has a stack. &nbsp;Maybe it does, but the comparison is rarely helpful. 

&nbsp;

 In this particular instance, the point I was trying to make was that spells in Magic need to distinct terms for &#39;playing&#39; and &#39;entering play&#39; because they use the stack to enter a limbo of &#39;will happen, but first giving you a chance to respond&#39; that SotM doesn&#39;t have. &nbsp;In SotM, the moment that you play a card it starts happening.
